= History of science bibliography
{parent=History of science}

= The Green Revolution, Peace, and Humanity by Norman Borlaug
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Norman Borlaug}
{title2=1970}
{title2=CIMMYT reprint, 1972}

https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1970/borlaug/lecture/
https://repository.cimmyt.org/entities/publication/73b44ad5-1fc1-4188-a413-0e68ddab5f60

The 1972 institutional reprint preserves the lecture delivered on 11 December 1970. It discusses agricultural production and population pressure, and rejects a miracle-variety account of the programme. The similarly named PDF currently served at the Nobel site's borlaug-lecture.pdf path is a different 2000 lecture and must not be silently cited as this one.

= Statement before Congress by Rachel Carson (1963)
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Rachel Carson}
{title2=1963}

https://rachelcarsoncouncil.org/about-rcc/about-rachel-carson/rachel-carsons-statement-before-congress-1963/

Contemporary report: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/cen-v041n024.p036

The report of the testimony distinguishes a continuing need for pesticides from the demand for tighter controls and stronger research. It should not be cited as advocacy of prohibiting every pesticide application.

= Kitāb nihāya al-sūl fī taṣḥīḥ al-uṣūl by Ibn al-Shatir
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Ibn al-Shatir}
{tag=Work by Erwan Penchèvre}
{title2=2017 critical edition, revised 2021}
{title2=arXiv:1709.04965}

https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.04965

Arabic text, French translation and mathematical commentary by <Erwan Penchèvre>. The introduction distinguishes the surviving exposition from the lost observational work and describes manuscript witnesses. The mathematical commentary gives modern reconstructions rather than attributing all of its proof notation to the original author.

= The Spherical Case of the Tūsī Couple by George Saliba and E. S. Kennedy
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by George Saliba}
{tag=Work by Edward Stewart Kennedy}
{title2=1991}
{title2=10.1017/S095742390000151X}

https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/arabic-sciences-and-philosophy/article/abs/spherical-case-of-the-tusi-couple/6CCCCA0CA68881F3E9EAB1F5A679E957

Original historical research, Arabic Sciences and Philosophy 1(2), pp. 285–291. The 2008 online publication date must not replace the 1991 issue date. The abstract distinguishes plane and spherical applications; access to the full article may require a subscription.

= Declaration of the United Nations Conference on the Human Environment
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1972}

= Stockholm Declaration
{synonym}
{c}

https://www.un.org/en/conferences/environment/stockholm1972
https://www.dipublico.org/987/stockholm-declaration-on-the-human-environment/

Primary declaration adopted at Stockholm. Principles 8–9 concern development; principle 16 addresses demographic policies and human rights.

= The Limits to Growth by Donella H. Meadows and others
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1972}

https://collections.dartmouth.edu/archive/text/meadows/diplomatic/meadows_ltg-diplomatic.html
https://www.clubofrome.org/publication/the-limits-to-growth/

Primary report by <Donella H. Meadows>, <Dennis L. Meadows>, <Jørgen Randers> and <William W. Behrens III>. Chapters I and III–V develop exponential growth, the world model, technological alternatives and stabilization scenarios. The graphs are conditional model runs.

= Impact of Population Growth by Paul R. Ehrlich and John P. Holdren
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1971}

https://doi.org/10.1126/science.171.3977.1212
https://mahb.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Impact-of-Population-Growth.pdf

Primary paper, Science 171, 1212–1217. Its opening decomposition is population multiplied by impact per person. A further division of per-capita impact into affluence and technology is an analytical decomposition, not a verbatim attribution of a three-factor formula to every part of this paper.

= Rachel Carson Answers Her Critics
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1963}

https://www.audubon.org/magazine/archives-rachel-carson-answers-her-critics

Primary speech reproduced from the September–October 1963 issue of Audubon. Documents continued wildlife impacts under officially organized spraying and objections raised by affected farmers.

= Road to Survival by William Vogt
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1948}

https://archive.org/details/roadtosurvival00vogt
https://doi.org/10.12987/9780300188479-018

Primary book; the DOI identifies a reproduced extract in The Future of Nature. Cited for the relationship between population, land degradation and ecological productive capacity. Access to the complete digitized book may require borrowing.

= An Essay on the Principle of Population by Thomas Robert Malthus
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1798}

https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/4239

Primary first-edition text. Chapters I–II contrast population increase with subsistence. This edition is a source for the population argument, not proof of which particular edition Darwin read.

= The Scientific Revolution by Steven Shapin
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1996}

https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/S/bo29143181.html

The Introduction contains the opening challenge to a unified Scientific Revolution. The publisher link describes the 2018 second edition, which retains that opening argument.

= Novum Organum by Francis Bacon
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1620}

https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45988/pg45988-images.html

Primary text in the English edition reproduced by Gutenberg. Book I, aphorisms 19, 38–44 and 95–105 are the cited methodological passages.

= Physics by Aristotle
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}

https://classics.mit.edu/Aristotle/physics.2.ii.html

Primary text in an English translation. Book II, chapters 1–3 distinguishes natural change, mathematical abstraction and the senses of cause.

= Babylonian astronomical diary VAT 4956
{parent=History of science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=568–567 BCE}

https://www.ebl.uni-muenchen.de/library/VAT.4956

Primary tablet edition. The dated record concerns the Moon and planets; it should not be confused with a later predictive procedure text.

